{"type":"standard","title":"Tangkhul language","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Tangkhul language</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q7682992","titles":{"canonical":"Tangkhul_language","normalized":"Tangkhul language","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Tangkhul language</span></span>"},"pageid":33232048,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/%22Tangkhul%22_written_in_Meitei_script.jpg/330px-%22Tangkhul%22_written_in_Meitei_script.jpg","width":330,"height":152},"originalimage":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/%22Tangkhul%22_written_in_Meitei_script.jpg","width":1690,"height":779},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1355640644","tid":"63a9cba0-5645-11f1-8b02-2ea76a6c9ebb","timestamp":"2026-05-23T01:19:05Z","description":"Sino-Tibetan language spoken in India","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangkhul_language","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangkhul_language?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangkhul_language?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Tangkhul_language"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangkhul_language","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Tangkhul_language","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangkhul_language?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Tangkhul_language"}},"extract":"Tangkhul is a Sino-Tibetan language of the Tangkhulic branch, spoken in different villages of Ukhrul district, Manipur, India. The term \"Tangkhul\" is derived from the Meitei language terms, tāng and khūl .\nAccording to another theory, the term \"Tangkhul\" is derived from \"Thankhul\", meaning \"Than village\" in Meitei language.","extract_html":"<p><b>Tangkhul</b> is a Sino-Tibetan language of the Tangkhulic branch, spoken in different villages of Ukhrul district, Manipur, India. The term \"Tangkhul\" is derived from the Meitei language terms, <span><i lang=\"nmf-Latn\">tāng</i></span> and <span><i lang=\"nmf-Latn\">khūl</i></span> .\nAccording to another theory, the term \"Tangkhul\" is derived from \"Thankhul\", meaning \"Than village\" in Meitei language.</p>"}